The standard approach in .NET is to **wrap file system calls behind an interface** and inject it:

```csharp
public interface IFileSystem
{
    string ReadAllText(string path);
    void WriteAllText(string path, string contents);
    bool DirectoryExists(string path);
}

public class PhysicalFileSystem : IFileSystem
{
    public string ReadAllText(string path) => File.ReadAllText(path);
    public void WriteAllText(string path, string contents) => File.WriteAllText(path, contents);
    public bool DirectoryExists(string path) => Directory.Exists(path);
}
```

Then inject `IFileSystem` into your classes. In tests, mock it or use an in-memory implementation.

**Alternatives:**

- **`System.IO.Abstractions`** — popular NuGet package that provides `IFileSystem` out of the box with a ready-made mock (`MockFileSystem`) for testing.
- **`Microsoft.Extensions.FileProviders.IFileProvider`** — lighter-weight, read-oriented abstraction from the framework itself.

For most projects, `System.IO.Abstractions` is the quickest path — install it, replace `File.*`/`Directory.*` calls with the injected abstraction, and use `MockFileSystem` in your tests with zero hand-rolled fakes.
